00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:35.362
When it comes to the muses of the animal kingdom,
the nematode seems like an unlikely well of inspiration.

00:00:35.362 --> 00:00:41.210
There may be poems about tigers burning bright,
and epic fantasy tales featuring giant birds.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:48.239
But nematodes have two obvious factors working
against them achieving that kind of mythical status.

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:50.210
First: they are worms.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:55.870
And let's not generalize too broadly, but their
simple tubular bodies are just not what most

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:00.019
people think of when it comes to the beauty
of the natural world.

00:01:00.019 --> 00:01:04.980
Second: most of them are really tiny, their
length measured in millimeters.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.710
It’s hard to be inspired by an animal that
is likely to escape one’s notice entirely.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:17.229
Though we should note that there are nematodes
that can grow quite long—the largest discovered

00:01:17.229 --> 00:01:25.090
was found in the placenta of a sperm whale,
and it measured between 8-9 meters in length.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:30.000
But even that seems more likely to inspire
more nightmares than art.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:37.550
In 1914, a scientist named Nathan Cobb wrote
the following about nematodes:

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:42.780
Nematodes do not furnish hides, horns, tallow,
or wool.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.115
They are not fit for food, they do not produce
anything fit to eat;

00:01:47.115 --> 00:01:51.991
neither do they sing or amuse us in any way; 
nor are they ornamental

00:01:51.991 --> 00:01:58.100
—in fact, when they are displayed in museums 
the public votes them hideous.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:03.192
Judging from that quote, Cobb seems to understand
the common consensus on nematodes,

00:02:03.192 --> 00:02:08.800
which is that if there is to be a consensus, 
it is not a positive one.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:16.979
But Cobb wrote this as part of a 34 page paper
on nematodes titled “Nematodes and their Relationships,”

00:02:16.979 --> 00:02:22.140
which wonderfully documents
his fascination with the worm and argues for

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.320
their importance to our understanding of the world.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.940
So clearly, he found something inspiring about
the nematode.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:33.820
But there is another quote from this research
paper that you may have even heard before,

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.980
it is pretty famous in nematode circles.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.380
And The quote is as follows:

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:45.210
In short, if all the matter in the universe
except the nematodes were swept away,

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:51.920
our world would still be dimly recognizable, 
and if, as disembodied spirits, we could then

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:59.500
investigate it, we should find its mountains,
hills, vales, rivers, lakes, and oceans represented

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.470
by a film of nematodes.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:10.742
Cobb paints a world overrun by nematodes, an image 
created by his own experience with studying them.

00:03:10.742 --> 00:03:19.130
But when he wrote this, he likely didn’t have the means
to estimate just how many nematodes there are in the world.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:27.240
But in 2019, researchers estimated that there
are about 57 billion nematodes in the world

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.000
for every human in the world.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.610
In addition, their total biomass is about
300 million tons.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.000
And that’s just for the soil nematodes.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.570
When you take into consideration nematodes
living in freshwater or marine habitats or

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:47.111
inside of other animals, the numbers only
go up from there.

00:03:47.111 --> 00:03:53.469
Nematodes are considered to be one of the
most abundant animals on earth.

00:03:53.469 --> 00:04:00.790
But the beauty of Cobb’s writing on their abundance 
is that he doesn’t just capture the notion of the numbers.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:07.135
The world he describes isn’t just a mass
of worms that we’ve been allowed space inside of.

00:04:07.135 --> 00:04:13.930
The world he describes—the one that we live
in—has its forms and landscapes sculpted

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:23.060
and cultivated by worms, their bodies shaped
around and inside trees and plants and animals.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:29.620
Of course, not all of those links between nematodes 
and other organisms are always so pleasant.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:35.108
In “Nematodes and their Relationships,”
Cobb mentions that he once removed over 40,000

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:37.960
nematodes from the stomach of a wallaby.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:42.810
And if that isn’t daunting enough to imagine,
there are always the ancient texts that date

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:48.790
back thousands of years, documenting intestinal
worms found in people.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:55.172
There are also the nematode eggs that have been 
found preserved in mummies and in fossilized poop.

00:04:55.172 --> 00:05:00.745
In the 19th century, scientists were able
to show that the parasite Ascaris lumbricoides

00:05:00.745 --> 00:05:05.296
which resides in the intestines and produces eggs
that are passed on through poop

00:05:05.296 --> 00:05:10.480
finds its way into people who have ingested their eggs
on contaminated food.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:16.120
To prove this, a scientist named Giovanni
Battista Grassi infected himself with their

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.585
eggs and then hunted for those eggs in his
feces.

00:05:20.585 --> 00:05:25.520
A peculiar aside here this is not the first
time we’ve described a scientist hunting

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.965
through his own poop for evidence of the microcosmos

00:05:28.965 --> 00:05:32.567
you can watch our Leeuwenhoek episode
for more tales of this type.

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:38.760
Now there are plenty of other 
nematode parasites that have

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:42.419
their own unique life cycles and horrifying effects.

00:05:42.419 --> 00:05:46.359
Some worms are transmitted through the soil,
others through insects.

00:05:46.359 --> 00:05:51.000
Some inflame the limbs, others trigger massive
blood loss.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.234
Nematodes can also parasitize plants, attacking
the roots or stem or flowers,

00:05:56.234 --> 00:06:00.173
sometimes destroying entire crops in the process.

00:06:00.173 --> 00:06:07.460
And so with such a destructive path, the nematode
feels like an enemy, something we study only

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.840
to understand how we can fight it.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.859
But Cobb asks a simple question in his writing:

00:06:14.859 --> 00:06:20.610
What would be our conception of the insect
group as a whole if our knowledge was largely

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:25.400
confined to these simple and 
degenerate parasitic  forms?

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:30.030
In the case of insects, we have enough experience
with them to know that whatever squeamishness

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.460
they inspire, they are also integral parts
of our world.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:38.600
And likewise most nematodes are actually free-living
species.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:43.820
It’s just that the parasites have come to
dominate both our imagination and our knowledge

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:47.590
because of their proximity and their consequences
for our health.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:53.303
But those massively abundant soil nematodes
they are vital to our world,

00:06:53.303 --> 00:06:56.180
feeding on bacteria and fungi.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:01.100
In the process, they release nitrogen back
into the soil, sustaining other organisms

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.178
and plants that may use it.

00:07:05.066 --> 00:07:11.590
A healthy soil is full of these free-living
nematodes, whose presence reflects the overall

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:14.010
diversity of microbes around them.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:20.526
The information is all there, it is up to
us to uncover their secrets.

00:07:20.526 --> 00:07:26.650
Perhaps this why Cobb ended “Nematodes and
their relationships” with this call to arms

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.350
for the field of nematology:

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:36.800
They offer an exceptional field of study;
and probably constitute among the last great

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:43.634
organic group worthy of a separate branch
of biological science comparable with entomology—

00:07:43.634 --> 00:07:45.634
nematology. 

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:53.960
We can’t speak to any claims that the nematode
is the quote “last great organic group”

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:55.930
worthy of its own branch of study.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:08:01.680
But we do feel that he has been vindicated
by the fact that one of the most popular model

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.569
organisms used today is a free-living nematode:

00:08:05.569 --> 00:08:10.280
Caenorhabditis elegans, better known as C.
elegans.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:14.400
While the worm is found in soils all over
the world, what it’s perhaps best known

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.180
for is the life it lives in labs.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:23.889
While scientists had described various aspects
of C. elegans life early in the 20th century,

00:08:23.889 --> 00:08:30.460
it wasn’t until 1965 that the scientist
Sydney Brenner turned to the worm as a model

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:34.727
organism, relying on its fast life cycle, small size,

00:08:34.727 --> 00:08:40.150
and capability to produce more
than 1,000 eggs a day.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:44.840
There were those who thought the worm’s
simplicity would render it useless in studies

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:47.090
of morphology and behavior.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:53.170
But time and technology has turned C. elegans
into a molecular muse.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:09:00.140
It has taught us about how life develops,
how human diseases progress, and how cells die.

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:05.640
When Cobb wrote, “My experience in this
matter makes me very confident in saying that

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:11.080
professors of biology could do far worse than
to introduce into their courses a more careful

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:16.640
examination of these creatures,” he didn’t
know that one day, many of those professors

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:20.750
of biology wouldn’t just be introducing
nematodes into their courses.

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:25.070
They would be introducing them into their
labs.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:32.810
When Cobb died in 1932, scientists didn’t
even know how genetic information was encoded in DNA.

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:37.956
More than 50 years later, C. elegans became
the first multicellular organism 

00:09:37.956 --> 00:09:40.540
to have its genome sequenced.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:45.820
And as it continues to teach us about our
world, NASA has even sent them to space

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:50.310
so we can learn about how life ages outside
of this planet.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:54.360
So maybe the nematode is not the muse we expected.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:58.050
But over the past century, they’ve become
one nonetheless.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:05.260
And perhaps that is the most fitting testament
to what a muse often actually is, a surprising

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:14.153
source of inspiration that seems to come from
nowhere, and then permeates until you see it everywhere.

